'''Botany''' is a New Zealand parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]], returning one Member of Parliament to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]]. It was contested for the first time at the [[2008 New Zealand general election|2008 general election]], and won by [[Pansy Wong]] for the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]]. Following Wong's resignation in late 2010, [[2011 Botany by-election|a by-election]] returned [[Jami-Lee Ross]], who was confirmed by the voters in the [[2011 New Zealand general election|2011 general election]]. Ross left the National Party in October 2018 and became an independent. Ross did not contest the seat at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020 general election]], and was succeeded by the new National candidate, [[Christopher Luxon]], who became the [[Leader of the New Zealand National Party|party's leader]] in November 2021 and has served as [[Prime Minister of New Zealand|prime minister]] since November 2023.

== Background ==
The [[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Representation Commission]] established the electoral district of Botany after the [[2006 New Zealand census]] due to high population growth in and around Auckland. The new electorate resulted from several sweeping changes to the electoral landscape of [[South Auckland]]:

* The southern end of [[Port Waikato (electorate)|Port Waikato]] was lanced and combined with the area around [[Clevedon, New Zealand|Clevedon township]] to form {{NZ electorate link| Hunua}}.
* The resultant change pulled the [[Clevedon (New Zealand electorate)|Clevedon electorate]] tighter around the city of [[Papakura]] for which [[Papakura (New Zealand electorate)|the electorate is now named]].
* In the north, [[Manukau East]] was pulled up through [[Otahuhu]] into [[Auckland City]], in the process dropping the western suburbs of [[Flat Bush]], [[East Tāmaki]], [[Dannemora, New Zealand|Dannemora]] and [[Botany Downs, New Zealand|Botany Downs]], which combined with fragments of the Clevedon and [[Pakuranga (New Zealand electorate)|Pakuranga]] electorates to form Botany.

==Population centres==
{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Botany.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
The electorate is located in [[East Auckland]] and includes the suburbs of [[Botany, New Zealand|Botany]], [[Botany Downs]], [[Somerville, New Zealand|Somerville]], [[Northpark, New Zealand|Northpark]],  [[Huntington Park, New Zealand|Huntington Park]], [[Golflands]], [[Burswood, New Zealand|Burswood]], [[Dannemora, New Zealand|Dannemora]], [[Shamrock Park, New Zealand|Shamrock Park]], [[East Tāmaki]], [[East Tāmaki Heights]], [[Highbrook]] and [[Flat Bush]]. In the 2025 boundary review, the electorate would gain [[Mission Heights]] from {{NZ electorate link|Takanini}}, and the coastal and rural communities of [[Whitford, New Zealand|Whitford]], [[Beachlands, New Zealand|Beachlands]] and [[Maraetai]] from {{NZ electorate link|Papakura}}, while transferring [[Cockle Bay, New Zealand|Cockle Bay]] and [[Shelly Park]] to {{NZ electorate link|Pakuranga}}, and [[Clover Park, New Zealand|Clover Park]] to {{NZ electorate link|Otahuhu}}.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=16 September 2025 |page=19 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>

==Demographics==
Demographically, Botany is older than the rest of New Zealand, with over half of its population aged over 30. [[Asian New Zealanders]] make up a plurality of the electorate (44.9%) nearly three times the national average, and nearly twice as many [[Pasifika New Zealanders]] (13 to 8.1%), which makes Botany a [[minority-majority]] electorate.  Botany has the highest proportion of people born overseas of any New Zealand electorate (53.9% in 2018), the most [[Buddhism in New Zealand|Buddhists]] in a New Zealand electorate, the lowest proportion of residents who do not speak English (10.8%) of the general electorates, and the highest number of one-family homes. The average income in the electorate is high, with over half of the electorate's residents earning over $50,000 a year.<ref name="parllib">{{cite web| url= https://www3.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/electorate-profiles/botany-electorate-profile/ |title=Botany - Electorate Profile |publisher= Parliamentary Library |date= 27 April 2021 |access-date= 21 September 2025}}</ref>

A 2005 academic survey assessing the voting behaviour of Asian New Zealanders<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.listener.co.nz/issue/3406/features/4538/asian_vote.html |work=New Zealand Listener | title=Asian Vote|date=2004-08-20}}</ref> showed a strong preference for the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]], with a sizeable proportion prepared to vote for the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]] (47 to 40); it also showed that among Asian New Zealanders, the most important issues were the economy and law and order. This was demonstrated by a large anti-crime march (a crowd of 15,000 was estimated, with a significant number of these being Asian New Zealanders) was held in Auckland (on the streets of Botany) in July 2008 amidst claims of increasing violent crime in New Zealand targeted against its Asian population.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://tvnz.co.nz/view/page/423466/1889878 |work=TVNZ News | title=Huge turnout for anti-crime march|date=2004-07-05}}</ref> The march's organiser Peter Low used his website to clarify his position, calling for harsher sentencing, victims' rights and zero "criminal rights".<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.aag.org.nz/ |work=Asian Anti-Crime Group Trust |title=ASIAN ANTI-CRIME GROUP (AAG) TRUST NEW ZEALAND |date=2004-08-05 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080905171914/http://www.aag.org.nz/ |archive-date=2008-09-05 }}</ref>

==History==
Botany was first created for the [[2008 New Zealand general election|2008 general election]], and won by [[Pansy Wong]] for the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]].<ref name="parllib"/>

Both National and the [[ACT New Zealand|ACT party]] stood Chinese New Zealanders as their candidates in 2008; [[Pansy Wong]] and [[Kenneth Wang (politician)|Kenneth Wang]], respectively. Labour chose Koro Tawa, an Auckland University lecturer. [[Raymond Huo]], a Chinese-speaking lawyer, was initially mooted for the Labour nomination, but eventually chose to stand as a list-only candidate.<ref>{{cite news|url= http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=10517403 |title=Botany a hot Asian battleground|publisher=[[The New Zealand Herald]]|date=20 June 2008|first=Lincoln|last=Tan}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.tewahanui.info/wordpress2/?p=394|title=Raymond Huo on Labour list|publisher=Te Waha Nui|first=Louise|last=Blockley|date=22 August 2008|access-date=2008-11-03|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110720150516/http://www.tewahanui.info/wordpress2/?p=394|archive-date=2011-07-20|url-status=dead}}</ref>

On 14 December 2010 it was announced that a [[2011 Botany by-election|by-election]] was to be held on 5 March 2011 due to the resignation of incumbent MP Pansy Wong.<ref name=resign>{{cite web |url= http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=10694214 |title=Pansy Wong resigns as MP |author=Cheng, Derek |date=14 December 2010 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |access-date=21 September 2011}}</ref> The electorate was won by [[Jami-Lee Ross]] from the [[New Zealand National Party]].

In October 2018, Jami-Lee Ross resigned from the National Party and accused party leader Simon Bridges of breaching electoral law. Ross announced his intention to resign from parliament and run as an independent in the resulting by-election,<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.radionz.co.nz/national/programmes/morningreport/audio/2018667150/jami-lee-ross-vows-to-fight-for-botany-seat|title=Jami-Lee Ross vows to fight for Botany seat|last=Morning Report|date=17 October 2018|website=RNZ}}</ref> however he later decided against resigning. [[Christopher Luxon]] won the National Party selection in November 2019.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wilson |first1=Simon |title=National selects Christopher Luxon as its new candidate for Botany |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=12282362 |access-date=5 November 2019 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=5 November 2019}}</ref>

On 15 September 2020 Ross announced he was no longer intending to contest the electorate, but will instead contest the upcoming election as a list only candidate for his newly formed party, [[Advance NZ]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Kirkness |first1=Luke |title=Election 2020: Advance NZ's Jami-Lee Ross abandons attempt to retain Botany seat |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=12364923 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=15 September 2020 |language=en-NZ|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200919005152/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=12364923|archive-date=19 September 2020|url-status=live}}</ref> Luxon won the seat at the {{NZ election link|2020}} as Ross was ejected from Parliament.

